From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] kbdsg, wine.inf: a keyboard layout DLL for the programs that load one (8703)

On Windows each keyboard layout is a DLL (KBDUS.DLL, KBDGR.DLL, ...) named by
HKLM\System\CurrentControlSet\Control\Keyboard Layouts\<KLID>\Layout File and
exporting KbdLayerDescriptor. GTK 3.24 and GTK 4 (GIMP, Inkscape, darktable,
Siril, ART, ...) read that value for each layout, load the DLL and map keys
to keysyms from its KBDTABLES. Wine has no layout DLLs and no Layout File:
GTK warned "Failed to translate keypress ... because we could not load the
layout" and lost every key that is not typed text -- Enter, Backspace, the
arrows, Ctrl+A, every shortcut (Siril's command line on qa4).

kbdsg.dll exports KbdLayerDescriptor and builds the tables of the layout
active in the calling thread (GTK activates each layout before loading its
DLL) from ToUnicodeEx, so they follow what the display driver made of the
keymap: Shift and AltGr levels, CAPLOK, dead keys (WCH_DEAD rows), VSC to VK.
Ctrl levels stay empty as in KBDUS (user32 makes control characters itself),
and an AltGr level only holds characters of its own, so Ctrl+Alt shortcuts
are not taken for characters. The 32-bit copy lays the tables out with
64-bit pointers, as syswow64's layout DLLs do (KBD_LONG_POINTERS). wine.inf
gives every layout winex11 builds (the language KLIDs of xkb_names and the
layout-id variants of 8212/8217) Layout File = KBDSG.DLL.
Mutant SG_MUTANT_KBDSG_NO_TABLES (KbdLayerDescriptor returns nothing).
